<h2>Purpose</h2>
<p>
  Grounds decision
  <a href="/backlog/2910-amend-2563-a-machine-park-is-the-only-available-spelling-of-/">#2910</a> — the
  proposed amendment to
  <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions/#blast-radius-advisory-care-not-a-gate">the blast-radius
  advisory-care rule</a> (#2563). Point 1 of that anchor says scored signals "do <strong>not</strong> block
  the land on a review verdict … the review still happens (via the loop), just not a human park." The shipped
  system contradicts that letter, and two attempts to reconcile the two by re-reading the anchor's text were
  struck. So the open question is a <strong>mechanism</strong> question, in three parts:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Is a <strong>label an automated reviewer sets and clears</strong> a legitimate way to express a merge
      hold, or is a label inherently the "human park" the anchor bars?</li>
  <li>Should such a hold be <strong>time-bounded</strong> — and what does the bound <em>do</em> when it
      fires?</li>
  <li>Is there a <strong>non-label</strong> alternative — a merge decision consulting an out-of-band store of
      review results — and is it better?</li>
</ol>
<p>
  This is new ground for this repo. The three existing reports
  (<code>2026-07-18-blast-radius-advisory-review-gating</code>,
  <code>2026-07-18-human-vs-ai-review-cognitive-science</code>,
  <code>2026-07-10-ai-code-review-best-practices</code>) settle <em>whether</em> to gate and <em>who</em>
  should judge; none of them surveys how a hold is spelled, who clears it, or whether it is bounded. The one
  bound our corpus already holds counts <strong>rounds, not elapsed time</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — a bot-set, bot-cleared blocking label is ordinary</h2>
<p>
  The load-bearing datum: <strong>Prow/tide's <code>needs-rebase</code> is applied <em>and removed</em> by a
  plugin with no human in the loop</strong> — a blocking label whose entire lifecycle is machine-owned. Prow
  additionally posts a status <em>context</em> purely to explain the hold: the label is the state, the status
  is the message. Kodiak keys auto-merge on a label; Palantir Bulldozer uses <code>WIP</code> /
  <code>Update Me</code> as blocker and trigger; Gerrit lets CI <em>service users</em> cast the
  <code>Verified</code> vote that submit requirements evaluate. The label is not the weak part of the design.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>System</th><th>What the hold is</th><th>Who sets / clears</th><th>Time-bounded?</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>GitHub commit statuses + required checks</td><td>commit status on a <code>context</code></td><td>any app/bot via REST</td><td><strong>No</strong> — a never-reported context blocks indefinitely</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GitHub merge queue</td><td>queue position + checks on the <code>merge_group</code> ref</td><td>CI/bot reports; queue clears</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — status-check timeout; "timed out awaiting a successful CI result" is a documented removal reason</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GitHub code-scanning merge protection</td><td>a <strong>ruleset rule</strong>, explicitly not a status check; read from the alert store</td><td>GitHub queries the store; the tool populates it out of band</td><td><strong>No</strong> — blocks while analysis "is still in progress"</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Prow / tide</td><td><strong>labels</strong> (<code>lgtm</code>, <code>approved</code>, <code>do-not-merge/hold</code>, <code>needs-rebase</code>) + an explanatory status</td><td><code>needs-rebase</code> is fully plugin-owned</td><td>No timeout; tide re-queries on a loop</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mergify</td><td>queue position, with <code>queue_conditions</code> naming labels/checks/approvals</td><td>bot or human sets; Mergify clears the slot</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — <code>checks_timeout</code>, default <code>auto</code> since 2026-06-29</td></tr>
