<h2>Finding 2 — the non-label alternative exists in production, and it is worse</h2>
<p>
  <strong>GitHub code-scanning merge protection</strong> is the one real instance of "the merge decision
  consults an external store of review results rather than a label on the PR." It is a ruleset rule,
  <em>explicitly not</em> a status check, evaluated against the code-scanning alert database, and it blocks
  both on findings above a severity threshold and on the analysis simply not being done yet. It has
  <strong>no timeout</strong>, and its best-documented failure mode is a permanent block waiting on a result
  that never posts (CodeQL default setup does not run on <code>merge_group</code>, so queued pull requests
  block forever — <code>github/codeql-action#1537</code>).
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Gerrit cuts the other way, and is the instructive case.</strong> Its strength is not that the record
  is a database rather than a label — it is that the review record and the merge decision live in
  <em>one</em> store (NoteDb), with submit requirements evaluated there. Prow/tide is the counter-example: it
  splits decider from store (tide decides; GitHub labels are the record), and its own documentation warns
  that GitHub's configured merge requirements must be kept in sync with tide's. That warning <em>is</em> the
  cost of splitting them. Going non-label therefore buys nothing and spends the single-source-of-truth
  property.
</p>

<h2>Finding 3 — the bound is real prior art, and its action is never "merge anyway"</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Plain GitHub required checks have <strong>no timeout</strong>. The documented seven-day rule expires a
      <em>success</em>; it does not bound a <em>pending</em>.</li>
  <li>Every system that <em>owns</em> a merge queue has added a wall clock: GitHub merge queue's status-check
      timeout, and Mergify's <code>checks_timeout</code>.</li>
  <li>Mergify's 2026-06-29 change is the most transferable design point: the default moved from off to
      <strong>auto</strong> — the p95 of recent successful CI runs plus a margin. <em>Derive the bound from
      what the clearer actually takes; don't guess a constant.</em></li>
  <li>In every surveyed case the fired timeout <strong>ejects and reports</strong>. GitLab drops a failed
      merge request off the train and re-pipelines behind it; Mergify dequeues; GitHub removes from queue.
      <strong>No surveyed system converts a timed-out hold into a merge.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>
  This reconciles cleanly with Web Everything's own ratified removal of the merge-anyway timer (#2425): what
  was struck there was a clock <em>whose action was to land</em> — "landing unreviewed code on a clock is
  never the right failure mode," pinned by a named invariant test. A clock whose action is to <em>escalate
  loudly</em> is a different mechanism, and is not what #2425 removed.
</p>

<h2>Cross-cutting — the advisory/blocking line is drawn on false-positive rate</h2>
<p>
  Google is the sharpest statement. Tricorder findings are <strong>advisory</strong> — grey comment boxes in
  Critique a developer may ignore — behind a numeric inclusion bar: an analyzer must produce
  <strong>fewer than 10% effective false positives</strong>, be understandable, be actionable, and have
  significant impact; the "Not useful" button is the measurement instrument, and analyzers that do not improve
  are put on probation and disabled. Blocking lives in a stricter tier: presubmits block a pending change, and
  the highest-confidence checks are promoted all the way to <strong>compiler errors</strong>, which require
  <em>no</em> effective false positives and must report correctness issues only. SonarQube draws the same
  line (a deterministic quality gate blocks; individual issues comment), as does GitHub's code-scanning
  severity threshold.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The implication for #2563:</strong> the anchor's advisory-vs-gate split is about <em>which signals
  may summon a reviewer</em> — the same axis the industry draws. Nothing in the prior art treats "a machine
  reviewer's verdict is a merge precondition" as the gated-risk-score side of that line. A bot-cleared hold is
  how the review is <em>delivered</em>, not a score being gated on.
</p>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Keep the label; bound the wait; make the bound escalate, never merge.</strong> The forks this
  supports, and the corrections it forces to the item's own premises, are stated on
  <a href="/backlog/2910-amend-2563-a-machine-park-is-the-only-available-spelling-of-/">#2910</a>.
</p>

