<h2>Limits — what this survey could not verify</h2>
<ul>
  <li>GitHub merge queue's <em>default</em> status-check timeout is not stated in the official documentation;
      a third-party figure of 60 minutes exists and is treated as unconfirmed.</li>
  <li>Mergify's exact behaviour when <code>checks_timeout</code> fires is inferred from the queue-rules page
      rather than restated in the changelog.</li>
  <li>No GitLab merge-train wall clock was found — absence of evidence, not evidence of absence.</li>
  <li>Prow/tide <code>lgtm</code> staleness on new commits is per-repository configurable and was not
      confirmed.</li>
  <li>The Tricorder ICSE'15 paper would not extract; its criteria are quoted via the
      <em>Software Engineering at Google</em> chapter-20 rendering of the same system.</li>
  <li>Zuul and Atlantis were surveyed more thinly than the rest; no per-change timeout was found for either,
      but their configuration references were not read exhaustively.</li>
</ul>
